      "reasoning": "FIRST includes the owner's confrontation and Center's consequential intervention, but omits the middle act of internal conflict. SECOND includes all three elements, although the check airman's explicit conflict appears after Center intervenes rather than as the second act.",
      "evidence": "SECOND: “The line between defending standard procedures and the respect for their superior’s experience has never been so blurred.”"
    },
    {
      "name": "rb_2",
      "first": 4,
      "second": 3,
      "reasoning": "FIRST explicitly establishes the owner as captain and senior pilot, identifies the subordinate check airman, and places the confrontation during a checkride. SECOND clearly portrays the airline owner at the controls and a subordinate check airman evaluating procedures, but never explicitly establishes that a checkride is underway.",
      "evidence": "FIRST: “I can't believe you had the nerve to criticize me during the checkride.” SECOND: “I built this airline from the ground up.”"
    },
    {
      "name": "rb_3",
      "first": 4,
      "second": 4,
      "reasoning": "Both justify bypassing the instructed VOR radial for direct flight to the FAF through efficiency and the frequency of receiving that clearance. FIRST uses the exact two-thirds rationale; SECOND uses the equivalent 'more often than not.'",
      "evidence": "FIRST: “Flying direct to the FAF instead of the VOR radial saves us time and fuel. They give it to us two-thirds of the time anyway!” SECOND: “We're saving time and fuel. I've flown this route a thousand times, and ATC clears us direct to the FAF more often than not.”"
    },
    {
      "name": "rb_4",
      "first": 1,
      "second": 3,
      "reasoning": "FIRST states the check airman's professional duty and concern for the airline's reputation, but does not depict an internal struggle against loyalty or employment pressure. SECOND explicitly describes tension between procedural responsibility and respect for the superior, although the loyalty dimension is less developed than the procedural one and job security is not explored.",
      "evidence": "FIRST: “we all have a duty to follow the rules and maintain our professional integrity.” SECOND: “The line between defending standard procedures and the respect for their superior’s experience has never been so blurred.”"
    },
    {
      "name": "rb_5",
      "first": 4,
      "second": 4,
      "reasoning": "Both have Center demand the heading in an explicitly angry or severe tone and directly condemn the deviation. SECOND escalates from stern urgency to an incensed response.",
      "evidence": "FIRST: “CENTER: (Over the radio, angry) [Airline Name] Flight [Number], say heading?” SECOND: “CENTER\n(Stern and urgent)\nFlight 429, say heading?”"
    },
    {
      "name": "rb_6",
      "first": 3,
      "second": 4,
      "reasoning": "FIRST directs the pilot to call a supplied placeholder number after landing about the violation, but neither explicitly identifies a pilot deviation report nor shows the number being copied. SECOND explicitly announces a potential pilot deviation, directs post-landing contact with a supervisor, and shows the check airman recording the number.",
      "evidence": "FIRST: “Contact us at [phone number] after landing to discuss this matter further.” SECOND: “Expect potential pilot deviation. Upon landing, contact the supervisor at this number...” and “the CHECK AIRMAN dutifully scribbles down.”"
    },
    {
      "name": "rb_7",
      "first": 4,
      "second": 3,
      "reasoning": "FIRST uses all the listed terminology, including flightdeck, checkride, VOR radial, FAF, and Center, appropriately for the scenario. SECOND appropriately uses flight deck, VOR radial, FAF, Center, and pilot deviation, but omits the checkride terminology and explicit evaluation context.",
      "evidence": "FIRST: “INT. FLIGHTDECK - DAY” and “criticize me during the checkride.” SECOND: “We need to adhere to the VOR radial as instructed.”"
    }
  ]
}

